## X thread

**1/**
Baseten's deepseek-ultrafast decodes DeepSeek-V4.1-Flash at {{ K.meas4.decode_tok_s|n }} tok/s on 4x GB300.

I modeled where the time goes. Only ~{{ (1 - K.fixed4_share)|pct }} of each 3.3 ms step moves weights. The other {{ K.fixed4_share|pct }} is fixed per-layer latency: kernel boundaries + all-reduces.

The HBM sits idle most of the step. 🧵 [step breakdown]

**2/**
That one fact explains their own numbers. 8x B300 is only {{ K.tp8_gain|pct }} faster per step than 4x GB300: more GPUs shrink the streaming part, not the {{ K.fixed4_ms|n(1) }} ms of latency.

Same reason Rubin's 2.75x bandwidth buys only ~{{ K.rubin_vs_gb300|pct }} with today's per-layer overheads (modeled).

**3/**
How I know: an op-level simulator built from the engine's Rust/CUDA source and the checkpoint's safetensors headers. It reproduces DeepSeek's 552B / 16B-active / 890 B-per-token KV exactly.

Calibrated on TP4, it predicts the TP8 step time to within {{ cal.loo.b300_tp8.error_pct|abs|n(1) }}%.

**4/**
So I designed silicon around the bottleneck: MDA-1, a decode-only ASIC with *the same* 8 TB/s HBM as a B300, a static compiled schedule (no kernels), weight prefetch, a hardware MoE router and in-fabric all-reduce.

Simulated: {{ C.asic.tok_s_user|n }} tok/s/user on 4 chips ({{ K.asic_vs_meas|x }}), {{ K.asic8|n }} on 8 ({{ K.asic8_vs_meas8|x }} the measured 8x B300). [strong scaling]

**5/**
It's not about FLOPs. MDA-1 carries 40% of a B300's compute. The win is converting bandwidth into tokens: HBM {{ C.asic.hbm_busy|pct }} busy vs {{ C.gpu_tp4.hbm_busy|pct }}.

Modeled {{ K.tpj_ratio|x }} tokens/J and {{ K.usd_ratio_owned|x }} lower $/M tokens than the same GPUs owned.

**6/**
Things that surprised me:
• A 128x128 systolic array runs decode GEMVs at 0.3-1.5% utilization (SCALE-Sim). The engine has to be 16 rows wide.
• Caching experts in SRAM is a dead end. It needs GBs per chip.
• Engram's 203 GB of tables need ~{{ (W.engram.lookup_bytes_per_step / (K.meas4.step_ms/1e3) / 1e6)|n(0) }} MB/s. Put them in LPDDR.

**7/**
Software headroom is real too: an ideal GPU stack bounds at {{ C.gpu_ideal_tp4.tok_s_user|n }} tok/s on the same 4 GPUs. Silicon pulls ahead as you scale out ({{ (K.asic8/K.ideal8)|x(2) }} at 8 chips) and on energy and cost.

Everything is open: simulator, calibration, tornado, ablation, RTL. `make reproduce`.
