## LinkedIn

Baseten open-sourced deepseek-ultrafast, a hand-tuned engine that decodes DeepSeek-V4.1-Flash at {{ K.meas4.decode_tok_s|n }} tokens/s per user on 4x GB300. I used it as the reference workload for a hardware study.

The headline: about {{ K.fixed4_share|pct }} of every decode step is fixed per-layer latency, not memory bandwidth. It explains why 8 GPUs are only {{ K.tp8_gain|pct }} faster than 4.

Designing around that, a decode-only ASIC with the same HBM bandwidth as a B300 projects {{ C.asic.tok_s_user|n }} tokens/s per user on 4 chips and {{ K.asic8|n }} on 8. That's {{ K.tpj_ratio|x }} the tokens per joule, from a statically scheduled pipeline, a hardware MoE router and in-fabric collectives.

Simulator, calibration against Baseten's published numbers, sensitivity analysis and a verified RTL block are in the repo, reproducible with one command. All MDA-1 numbers are modeled projections.

## Reply to the Baseten team

Hi Baseten team, thanks for open-sourcing deepseek-ultrafast. It's the most legible serving engine I've read, and it made a serious hardware study possible.

I built an op-level performance model from the engine's source and V4.1's checkpoint headers. Calibrated to your README's step times (3.30 ms TP4, 2.71 ms TP8), it predicts one configuration from the other within ~{{ [cal.loo.gb300_tp4.error_pct|abs, cal.loo.b300_tp8.error_pct|abs]|max|n(1) }}%.

It says about {{ K.fixed4_ms|n(1) }} ms of each 3.30 ms step is fixed per-layer latency: roughly {{ (K.fixed4_ms*1000/43)|n(0) }} µs per block across ~13 dependent kernels and 2 all-reduces. HBM averages ~{{ pct(W.decode_per_gpu_tp4.total/1e9/K.meas4.step_ms/8.0) }} of peak over the step.

Two things you might find useful:

1. The model puts the software ceiling on the same 4x GB300 at about {{ C.gpu_ideal_tp4.tok_s_user|n }} tok/s: persistent kernel, cross-layer L2 prefetch of the next layers' attention/shared weights while experts stream, and a ~2.4 µs all-reduce. The prefetch piece is the least obvious. Next-layer dense weights can stream during the current layer's gate→expert gap.
2. One `nsys` run through your `tools/nsys_breakdown.py` would confirm or kill the diagnosis. I'd love to compare notes if you're up for sharing a breakdown.

The ASIC side, MDA-1, is a simulated projection: {{ C.asic.tok_s_user|n }} tok/s/user on 4 chips at iso-HBM bandwidth. Everything is in the repo: https://github.com/benblaine/dsv41-accel-study
